beacond for Debian
===================

The beacond.service unit is installed, enabled, AND started automatically on
install. This is a deliberate departure from daemons that ship inactive by
default: beacond needs no site configuration to run correctly (the packaged
/etc/beacond/beacond.yaml already matches its built-in defaults -- listen
127.0.0.1:9911, log_level info) and depends on nothing external -- no
credentials, no other host. If you don't want it running immediately, stop
and disable it right after install:

  systemctl disable --now beacond

To change the listen address or log level, edit /etc/beacond/beacond.yaml
(a normal dpkg conffile -- your edits are preserved across upgrades) and:

  systemctl restart beacond

Upgrades restart the service automatically (it is stateless, so this drops
nothing in flight); see debian/rules if you need to change that policy.

The beacon system user and its directories (/var/lib/beacond, the account's
home; /var/log/beacond, for rotated log archives) are created automatically
via systemd-sysusers / systemd-tmpfiles on install, and are never removed by
this package, including on purge.

 -- beacond maintainers <beacond@example.com>
